# JSON Schema “meta‑schema” and $-prefixed keywords

Meta‑schema = the schema that defines what a JSON Schema looks like.
Example: "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema" tells validators which dialect/keywords to use.

$ keywords are JSON Schema control keywords, not your data fields:

"$schema": which draft/vocabulary to use.

"$id": canonical identifier/URI for the schema (helps with $ref resolution).

"$ref": reference another schema by URI/ID (reuse).

"$defs" (or older $definitions): local named subschemas.

"$anchor", "$vocabulary", "$dynamicRef"… advanced, rarely needed for our use case.

## Why use them in envelope.schema.json?

"$schema": makes tool behavior consistent (we’ve chosen 2020‑12).

"$id": lets other schemas $ref the envelope cleanly.

"$defs": if we factor shared bits (e.g., an Id or Meta object).

We do not put $ in our message fields; $ is reserved for schema control.

## Minimal envelope schema head (what we use)

{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "$id": "envelope.schema.json",
  "type": "object",
  "required": ["id", "ver", "name", "ts", "meta", "payload"],
  ...
}

## Origins

Around 2010–2012, the JSON Schema project began as a community effort to define a standard way to describe and validate JSON documents.

The domain json-schema.org was set up as the official home of the specification drafts and documentation.

## Governance

JSON Schema is not an ISO or W3C standard.

It is published as an IETF Internet-Draft series (not yet an RFC).

Current drafts are named like draft-handrews-json-schema-01 (after the editor).

These drafts are recognized but not on the standards track at IETF (so not an RFC 8xxx).

The project is managed by the JSON Schema Organization, a group of maintainers (not a formal standards body).

Their GitHub: https://github.com/json-schema-org

They publish the specs and host tooling docs on json-schema.org.

## Status

Widely adopted in tooling (OpenAPI, Swagger, AJV, FastAPI/Pydantic internally, etc.).

Because it’s not an RFC, some specs call it “informal standard” or “de facto standard”.

Drafts:

Draft-04 (2013) → widely supported, still in use.

Draft-07 (2018).

Draft 2019-09.

Draft 2020-12 (current).

Validators usually support multiple drafts.

## Why it matters for us

When we put "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema" at the top of a schema, we’re telling validators “use the 2020-12 dialect defined by the JSON Schema Org group, per IETF draft”.

That string isn’t magic — it’s just a URI identifier. But most validators recognize it and load the correct vocabulary.

In one sentence

json-schema.org is the community-managed home of the IETF Internet-Draft “JSON Schema” specification — not ISO/W3C, but the accepted de facto standard, maintained by a working group of volunteers and tool authors.